# Stage 4 - Performance Improvement

## Problem Statement

The notification system currently experiences performance issues when a student has over 100,000 notifications.

Challenges:

* Slow page loading
* Increased database queries
* High memory usage
* Poor user experience

---

## Solution 1: Pagination

Instead of loading all notifications at once, load notifications in smaller batches.

Example:

```http id="bvxejl"
GET /notifications?page=1&limit=20
```

Benefits:

* Reduced response size
* Faster API responses
* Lower memory consumption

---

## Solution 2: Redis Caching

Store frequently accessed notifications in Redis.

Architecture:

```text id="akg4wk"
Frontend
   |
   v
Redis Cache
   |
   v
Notification Service
   |
   v
PostgreSQL
```

Benefits:

* Faster read operations
* Reduced database load
* Improved scalability

Tradeoff:

* Additional infrastructure cost
* Cache invalidation complexity

---

## Solution 3: WebSockets

Use WebSocket connections to push notifications in real time.

Workflow:

```text id="8wfysd"
Notification Created
        |
        v
WebSocket Server
        |
        v
Connected Students
```

Benefits:

* Real-time updates
* No continuous polling
* Better user experience

Tradeoff:

* Persistent connections consume resources

---

## Solution 4: Infinite Scrolling

Load notifications as the user scrolls.

Benefits:

* Faster initial page load
* Reduced bandwidth usage
* Improved responsiveness

---

## Solution 5: Lazy Loading

Load notification details only when required.

Benefits:

* Reduced memory usage
* Faster rendering

---

## Recommended Architecture

```text id="kg1j33"
React Frontend
        |
        v
API Gateway
        |
        v
Redis Cache
        |
        v
Notification Service
        |
        v
PostgreSQL Database
```

---

## Conclusion

Combining pagination, Redis caching, WebSockets, infinite scrolling, and lazy loading significantly improves performance and scalability for large-scale notification systems.
